1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">Comparing collider measurements with theory
requires unfolding, that is,</font>

2
00:00:05,128 --> 00:00:09,289
<font color="#FFFFFF">correcting the distortions that detectors introduce
into the data.</font>

3
00:00:09,369 --> 00:00:13,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">But most unfolding methods first bin the data into
histograms,</font>

4
00:00:13,354 --> 00:00:17,837
<font color="#FFFFFF">while many theory predictions live at the level of
statistical moments.</font>

5
00:00:17,917 --> 00:00:20,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces Moment Unfolding,</font>

6
00:00:20,424 --> 00:00:26,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">a machine-learning method that directly unfolds
distribution moments without ever binning.</font>

7
00:00:26,208 --> 00:00:32,363
<font color="#FFFFFF">Inspired by Generative Adversarial Networks and by
Boltzmann's approach to statistical mechanics,</font>

8
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:38,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">it recovers moments to sub-percent accuracy on both
Gaussian toy data and simulated LHC jets.</font>

9
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">Unfolding, also known as deconvolution,</font>

10
00:00:41,124 --> 00:00:46,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">corrects the distortions a detector imprints on
measured data so that experiments can</font>

11
00:00:46,362 --> 00:00:48,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">be compared with each other and with theory.</font>

12
00:00:49,074 --> 00:00:54,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">The usual recipe unfolds an entire spectrum after
first discretizing it into a histogram,</font>

13
00:00:54,559 --> 00:00:57,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">then computes moments from that histogram.</font>

14
00:00:57,147 --> 00:01:00,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">But this binning step introduces discretization artifacts,</font>

15
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:06,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it is wasteful when the quantity you actually
care about is just a small set of moments</font>

16
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:08,469
<font color="#FFFFFF">as a function of another observable.</font>

17
00:01:08,549 --> 00:01:14,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">That mismatch between binned data and moment-level
theory predictions is the gap this paper closes.</font>

18
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:18,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">Summarizing a distribution with a few moments makes
it tractable to visualize and,</font>

19
00:01:18,803 --> 00:01:22,577
<font color="#FFFFFF">crucially, to predict from first principles.</font>

20
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:26,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">For example,</font>

21
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:30,283
<font color="#FFFFFF">the full densities of hadronic jets cannot be
computed in perturbative QCD,</font>

22
00:01:30,363 --> 00:01:34,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the energy dependence of their moments can be.</font>

23
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:37,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">Unbinned unfolding methods already exist and avoid
binning artifacts,</font>

24
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:41,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they are built to unfold entire spectra,</font>

25
00:01:41,923 --> 00:01:45,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they may trade away precision on the handful of
moments a physicist actually wants.</font>

26
00:01:45,777 --> 00:01:49,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">This motivates a dedicated method that unfolds the
moments themselves.</font>

27
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,547
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core contribution is Moment Unfolding,</font>

28
00:01:52,627 --> 00:01:56,078
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new unbinned and non-iterative reweighting technique.</font>

29
00:01:56,158 --> 00:02:00,573
<font color="#FFFFFF">It learns a reweighting function, playing the role
of a GAN generator,</font>

30
00:02:00,653 --> 00:02:06,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">whose form is inspired by the Boltzmann factor so
that its trainable parameters can be</font>

31
00:02:06,174 --> 00:02:09,305
<font color="#FFFFFF">directly identified with the observable's moments.</font>

32
00:02:09,385 --> 00:02:14,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">A discriminator pushes the reweighted simulation to
match the target data.</font>

33
00:02:14,136 --> 00:02:18,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">Unlike OmniFold, which trains a fresh pair of
networks on every iteration,</font>

34
00:02:18,888 --> 00:02:22,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">Moment Unfolding trains a single pair of networks
just once.</font>

35
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:27,278
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method borrows Boltzmann's idea of building the
distribution that maximizes entropy subject to fixed moments.</font>

36
00:02:27,358 --> 00:02:31,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">Concretely,</font>

37
00:02:31,676 --> 00:02:35,914
<font color="#FFFFFF">the generator is written as the exponential of a
polynomial in the observable,</font>

38
00:02:35,994 --> 00:02:40,232
<font color="#FFFFFF">so its coefficients, the lambdas, are the moments
being unfolded.</font>

39
00:02:40,312 --> 00:02:44,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">This generator reweights the simulated events,</font>

40
00:02:44,630 --> 00:02:48,868
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a discriminator neural network tries to tell
the reweighted simulation apart from the real data.</font>

41
00:02:48,948 --> 00:02:53,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">The two are trained against each other on a
weighted binary cross-entropy loss: the discriminator</font>

42
00:02:53,266 --> 00:02:57,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">minimizes it while the generator maximizes it.</font>

43
00:02:57,584 --> 00:03:01,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the reweighting only changes importance
weights and not the event features,</font>

44
00:03:01,902 --> 00:03:06,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">the expensive detector emulation runs a single time.</font>

45
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,856
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is tested on two problems.</font>

46
00:03:08,936 --> 00:03:12,382
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, a Gaussian toy: the truth is a standard normal,</font>

47
00:03:12,462 --> 00:03:15,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">the generation is shifted to mean minus one-half,</font>

48
00:03:15,662 --> 00:03:18,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the detector adds wide Gaussian noise,</font>

49
00:03:18,404 --> 00:03:22,699
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a million samples split three to one for
training and testing.</font>

50
00:03:22,779 --> 00:03:25,964
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because a Gaussian has only finitely many moments,</font>

51
00:03:26,044 --> 00:03:30,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">unfolding its moments is equivalent to unfolding
the whole density.</font>

52
00:03:30,419 --> 00:03:35,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, hadronic jets from simulated LHC
collisions, using the jet width observable,</font>

53
00:03:35,904 --> 00:03:41,505
<font color="#FFFFFF">drawn from the same Pythia and Herwig plus Delphes
datasets used in the OmniFold paper,</font>

54
00:03:41,585 --> 00:03:46,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">where one simulation stands in for data and the
other for the synthetic reference.</font>

55
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:49,522
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are strong on both tasks.</font>

56
00:03:49,602 --> 00:03:54,311
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the Gaussian example, the loss function peaks
exactly at the true mean,</font>

57
00:03:54,391 --> 00:03:57,375
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming the method recovers the right answer,</font>

58
00:03:57,455 --> 00:04:00,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the discriminator converges within about ten epochs.</font>

59
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:06,058
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the jet width, the team unfolds the first and
second moments simultaneously.</font>

60
00:04:06,138 --> 00:04:13,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scanning the loss as a function of each candidate
moment produces curves whose peaks land on the true values,</font>

61
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:17,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a mean absolute error of two hundredths of a
percent or better.</font>

62
00:04:17,439 --> 00:04:21,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">That is sub-percent agreement between the unfolded
and true moments.</font>

63
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:27,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">One instructive observation concerns the limits of
unfolding only a couple of moments.</font>

64
00:04:27,414 --> 00:04:32,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">After Moment Unfolding matches the first and second
moments of the jet width,</font>

65
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:38,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">the full distributions of truth and reweighted
generation still are not statistically identical.</font>

66
00:04:38,144 --> 00:04:43,399
<font color="#FFFFFF">The reason is simply that higher moments remain
relevant and were not part of the fit.</font>

67
00:04:43,479 --> 00:04:45,011
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is expected behavior,</font>

68
00:04:45,091 --> 00:04:50,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it clarifies that the technique deliberately
controls the specific moments you ask for,</font>

69
00:04:50,735 --> 00:04:52,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving the rest free.</font>

70
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

71
00:04:54,712 --> 00:05:02,130
<font color="#FFFFFF">The unfolded moments agree with the true moments to
within two hundredths of a percent mean absolute error.</font>

72
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:05,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two moments of the jet width are unfolded at once.</font>

73
00:05:05,714 --> 00:05:08,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">The discriminator converges within ten epochs.</font>

74
00:05:08,937 --> 00:05:14,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">The Gaussian study uses a million samples with a
three-to-one train-test split,</font>

75
00:05:14,473 --> 00:05:21,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the entire set of notebooks reproduces in under
five minutes on a single Nvidia RTX6000 GPU.</font>

76
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:26,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that you can unfold detector
effects directly at the level of moments,</font>

77
00:05:26,904 --> 00:05:28,709
<font color="#FFFFFF">without ever binning the data.</font>

78
00:05:28,789 --> 00:05:34,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">Moment Unfolding does this with a GAN-like
generator whose parameters are the moments,</font>

79
00:05:34,194 --> 00:05:36,564
<font color="#FFFFFF">trains only once rather than iterating,</font>

80
00:05:36,644 --> 00:05:43,037
<font color="#FFFFFF">and recovers the true moments to better than a
hundredth of a percent on realistic LHC jet simulations.</font>

81
00:05:43,117 --> 00:05:46,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the algorithm is agnostic to the dataset,</font>

82
00:05:46,196 --> 00:05:51,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">the same idea could carry over to deconvolution
problems well beyond particle physics.</font>
